      "title": "User delete route lacks admin check",
      "description": "The DELETE /users/:id route omits the requireAdmin middleware that its sibling routes use. Any caller can reach db.removeUser with an arbitrary id. Any client that reaches the server can delete any user account. The attacker can enumerate ids to wipe the user table. Add requireAdmin to the route, as the GET and purge routes do.",
      "suggested_change": "admin.delete(\"/users/:id\", requireAdmin, async (req, res) => {",
      "source": "lens:id-enumeration-sequential",
      "candidate": null,
      "notes": [],
      "problem": "The DELETE /users/:id route omits the requireAdmin middleware that its sibling routes use. Any caller can reach db.removeUser with an arbitrary id.",
      "consequence": "Any client that reaches the server can delete any user account. The attacker can enumerate ids to wipe the user table.",
      "fix": "Add requireAdmin to the route, as the GET and purge routes do."
    },
    {
      "origin": "agent",
      "severity": "major",
      "category": "bug",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "file_path": "src/admin.js",
      "line_number": 22,
      "line_end": 25,
      "title": "Purge deletes run as floating promises",
      "description": "The forEach callback returns promises that the purge handler never awaits or catches. The handler reports ids.length as removed before any delete finishes. db.removeUser throws for an unknown id, so one bad id causes an unhandled rejection that crashes the Node process. The removed count is also wrong when deletes fail. Await the deletes with Promise.allSettled and report the number that succeeded.",
      "suggested_change": "const results = await Promise.allSettled(ids.map((id) => db.removeUser(id)));\n  res.json({ removed: results.filter((r) => r.status === \"fulfilled\").length });",
      "source": "lens:async-floating-promise",
      "candidate": null,
      "notes": [],
      "problem": "The forEach callback returns promises that the purge handler never awaits or catches. The handler reports ids.length as removed before any delete finishes.",
      "consequence": "db.removeUser throws for an unknown id, so one bad id causes an unhandled rejection that crashes the Node process. The removed count is also wrong when deletes fail.",
      "fix": "Await the deletes with Promise.allSettled and report the number that succeeded."
    },
    {
